the plight of the murakarpan family has  made headlines around the world their  bid to seek asylum and settle in  australia was denied in 2018 and the  government attempted to deport them that  triggered a public outcry and a  community in the small rural town of  billauela where the family had been  living ran a social media campaign to  protect them we want to just bring this  family in just to you know and until  they settled again we have schools that  are ready to welcome those two little  girls  so our whole town  just wants this nightmare to be over  on thursday the family's legal fight to  remain in australia returned to the  federal circuit court in melbourne  mother priya father nadez and sister  coppica are now on bridging visas in  community detention in perth  four-year-old tarnica's visa has not  been granted the immigration minister  alex burke has now granted the parents  and eldest daughter a three-month  extension to their bridging visas while  he considers their case  they're at great risk really of  potentially being redetained in an  immigration facility  or otherwise perhaps being put into  community detention or worst case  scenario would be that  they would possibly be deported from  australia  deportation poses risks according to the  tamil refugee groups who argue those who  fled the military and returned to sri  lanka could be targeted  because of the publicity that they have  received  there is a higher risk of them  being  subjected to  aras um arbitrary detention uh you know  forced disappearance  ledez and priya muragapan fled sri lanka  arriving illegally by boat to australia  in 2012 and 2013. they met and started a  family while their applications for  asylum was being assessed the federal  government says anyone who comes by boat  should not be granted asylum  applications for the protection of this  family have all been rejected and their  appeals through the high court and the  federal court have also failed the  future of the muragapan family now lies  in the hands of australia's immigration  minister he's expected to make a  decision on their visas by christmas  sarah clark al jazeera brisbane  australia
 

